# Rust
**/target/
**/*.rs.bk
*.pdb

# Dependencies
Cargo.lock

# IDE
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Claude Code
.claude/
CLAUDE.md

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Build artifacts
*.so
*.dylib
*.dll
*.exe

# The website itself lives in the biolang-website repository. What appears here
# is a local build/checkout copy: nothing under it is tracked, and staging it
# would add tens of megabytes of generated HTML and node_modules.
/website/

# mdBook output (HTML generated by CI — ignore everything except PDFs)
/book/output/
/website/practice/*
!/website/practice/bioinformatics-in-practice.pdf

# Active books — keep built output (tracked for GitHub Pages)
!/website/books/practical-bioinformatics/pdf/output.pdf

# Old/inactive book HTML outputs
/website/books/biostatisticsold/
/website/biostatistics/
/website/clinical-genomics/
/website/data-wrangling/
/website/funcgenomics/
/website/genomic-visualization/
/website/proteomics-structural/
/website/reproducible-pipelines/
/website/sequence-analysis/

# Practice book downloaded data
/practice/data/*
!/practice/data/.gitignore

# Benchmark generated data, environments, and timestamped results
/benchmarks/data/
/benchmarks/data_real/
/benchmarks/data_large/
/benchmarks/data_large_real/
/benchmarks/correctness/real_data/
/benchmarks/.venv/
/benchmarks/.RData
/benchmarks/.Rhistory

# Keep latest results, ignore timestamped runs
/benchmarks/results/*
!/benchmarks/results/latest/

# Books — ignore all except biostatistics, language, practical-bioinformatics
/books/*
!/books/biostatistics/
!/books/language/
!/books/practical-bioinformatics/
!/books/single-cell-rna-seq/
!/books/msmb/
!/books/hbc-scrnaseq/

# The MSMB companion generates its own data, so nothing here needs committing.
# Readers who want the authors' original bundle download it into this directory;
# it is theirs to host and would add tens of megabytes here.
/books/msmb/data/

# SVGs the chapter 3 scripts write when you run them. Regenerated on demand.
/books/msmb/code/ch03/figures/

# ...and its built HTML, 1.3 MB of generated output. pages.yml builds this book
# from source on every deploy, so the published copy comes from CI and a
# committed one would only add repository size and a second thing to keep in
# sync. The other books commit theirs for historical reasons; this is the
# arrangement to prefer.
/website/books/msmb/

# Node modules (website build)
node_modules/

# Optional isolated environment for Scanpy cross-validation
/.venv-scanpy/
/packages/.venv-scanpy/

# Fixtures and outputs generated by the book and package examples. The
# single-cell book tells readers to create these inside packages/.
/packages/nsclc_like/
/packages/validation_data/
/packages/singlecell-results/
/packages/singlecell-examples/
/packages/*.svg
/packages/*.csv
/packages/fragments.tsv.gz
/packages/genes.gtf
/packages/truth.csv
# Metrics written by the cross-validation examples, which now live in the
# biolang-workflows repository.
/packages/validation_*.json
**/__pycache__/

# Example pack artifacts. Both are generated from packs/*/pack.toml — the
# manifests are the source of truth, so the built bundles are never committed.
/dist/packs/
/website/packs/
# The built workbench is staged here by the deploy, which wipes the directory
# first. REQUIREMENTS.md is tracked source that moved here with the Studio
# rename, so it needs an exception or the ignore rule fights the index.
/website/workbench/*
!/website/workbench/REQUIREMENTS.md

# Pack archives are generated and their bytes depend on the checkout line
# endings, so a Windows and a Linux build produce different files from identical
# sources. The .html, .bln and .bl beside them are text and stay committed.
/website/docs/examples/*.zip

# Written by the doc-example sweep for the CLI checker to consume.
/website/tests/cli-only-blocks.json

# Benchmark inputs are generated from a seed by bench/harness.py.
/bench/data/

# Scratch output. Three of these had been committed — an empty file named after
# a fragment of a JS snippet, a dump of every builtin name, and the .bln output
# of an .Rmd conversion. Nothing referenced any of them.
*.tmp
/test_notebook.*

# Files the documentation examples write into whatever directory they are run
# from. Running the doc sweep at the repository root leaves all of these beside
# the sources, where they read as new project files rather than as output — the
# Dockerfile below, for instance, is written by docs/cookbook/containers.html.
# Every one of them is reproducible by re-running the example that emits it.
/*.svg
/*.tsv
/*.bed
/*.sam
/*.zarr/
# Examples write their own .zarr before reading it back, so a committed store is
# a byproduct wherever it lands, not just at the repository root.
*.zarr/
/Dockerfile
/_params.json
/results.json
/output.txt
/significant_genes.txt
/gene_annotations.csv
/gene_summary.csv
/protein_comparison.csv
/output/
/results/

# Scratch logs and caches written by notebook-server and development runs.
/tmp/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Never commit these. The split to biolang-workflows dropped these rules, and
# without them a plain `git add -A` in this repository stages 1,508 files of
# GPL-3 R packages and the study authors' raw 10x data into an MIT tree.
#
# The isolated R library used by the external validation oracle. Its contents
# are GPL-3 (glmGamPoi, DelayedMatrixStats, HDF5Array and others); BioLang is
# MIT and must not carry them.
/.validation-r-library/
# Oracle outputs and comparison artifacts. Large, regenerable, and they belong
# with the harness in biolang-workflows.
/validation-results/
# The HBC course data. It belongs to the study authors and the training team
# hosts it; a copy here would be a redistribution as well as gigabytes.
/ctrl_raw/
/stim_raw/
